"""Apply the outside review of the RESS draft: alarm definitions, four-failure framing, calibration, onset, decision rules, conclusions. Run after patch_sciencedirect_ress.py and alarm_defs.py."""
from pathlib import Path
p=Path("submission-ress/manuscript.tex");T=p.read_text()
def between(a,b):
    i=T.index(a);j=T.index(b,i);return T[i:j]
R=[(between("We tested transfer on the public","\\end{abstract}"),
"We tested transfer on the public MetroPT-3 metro compressor data: 1,516,948 records but only four reported air leaks. A chronological 60/20/20 split left no reported failure in its test partition, so each failure was held out in turn at horizons from 30 minutes to 12 hours. We compared logistic, gradient boosting, long short-term memory and temporal convolutional models with an operating-state-only model under rules fixed in advance. No horizon met the rules. Alarms caught none of 250 failure tests, which reuse those four failures across models, horizons and analyses. That includes 50 forward-only tests trained on earlier data. Four further seeds caught 3 of 280. Platt scaling collapsed towards the base rate. Median discrimination for boosting ranged from 0.15 to 0.51, against 0.97 to 1.00 on a random row split, although sequence models fitted their training failures closely. Moving labels one hour later, into the reported failure, raised median discrimination to 0.88 or above at 30 minutes, with catches nearly all after the reported start: detection, not warning. Results describe one compressor and support no risk claim.\n"),
("\\item It reports non-transfer: no alarm preceded a failure in 250 failure tests, and","\\item It reports non-transfer: no alarm preceded a failure in 250 failure tests, which reuse the four reported failures, and"),
("An alarm would prompt an inspection of the air production unit, and that takes time to arrange. An alarm therefore counts only if it comes at least 5 minutes before the reported start.","An alert would ask the maintenance team to inspect the air production unit, and that takes time to arrange. A catch therefore counts only if it comes at least 5 minutes before the reported start."),
("Analyses C and D are forward-only. Analysis E","Analyses C and D are forward-only. Holding out a whole failure tests transfer between episodes, but it lets a model train on failures that came later. Training only on earlier data is the closer test of prospective deployment, and here it rests on one training failure. Analysis E"),
("Where $b = 0$, every prediction in the fold is one probability and the post-calibration slope is undefined.","The fit constrains $b$ to be nonnegative, so it cannot invert a score whose direction reverses, and it returns $b = 0$ when the calibration scores show no positive relationship. The coefficient $b$ is fitted on calibration scores and is distinct from the slope $\\beta$, which is estimated on held-out decisions. Where $b = 0$, every prediction in the fold is one probability and $\\beta$ is undefined."),
("An alarm needs three of five decisions above the 99.5th percentile of calibration-role negative scores, with one-hour merging. Each catch is compared with circular shifts of the alarm sequence, as in \\cite{chikane2026}.",
"A persistence signal is on when three of the last five valid decisions exceed the 99.5th percentile of calibration-role negative scores. A notification, which is what a maintainer would receive, is issued when the signal is on and at least 60 minutes have passed since the last notification. As registered, a catch means the signal is on at some time from the horizon before the reported start to 5 minutes before it. A signal already on when that window opens can therefore earn a catch without a new notification. False alarms, in contrast, count notifications, per monitored day: scored minutes, including those with no valid lookback, when no decision can be issued. Each catch is compared with circular shifts of the signal, as in \\cite{chikane2026}.\n\nBecause those two measures differ, we recounted every analysis from saved scores, under a plan dated before the recount. A catch was also counted only when a new notification starts in the window, and false alarms were also expressed per decision day, using only minutes with a valid decision. The registered definitions stay primary."),
("These numeric limits were not set by a maintenance engineer, so outcomes are provisional.","In plain terms, each false alarm is a request to inspect a unit that the reports do not show to be failing, so the burden limit allows up to one such request a day. The 5-minute lead is a floor that denies credit to alarms at or after the start, not an estimate of how long an inspection takes to arrange, and the longer horizons show what more notice would require. These numeric limits are study rules and were not set by a maintenance engineer, so outcomes are provisional. Passing the burden limit means only that alerts were few, which can reflect silence, and it says nothing about maintenance benefit. We measured alarm burden and modelled no costs or savings."),
("No registered alarm preceded a failure with the required lead in any of 250 failure tests across all folds and models (150 for M1 to M4 and 100 for M5 and M6).","No registered alarm preceded a failure with the required lead in any of 250 failure tests across all folds and models (150 for M1 to M4 and 100 for M5 and M6). These tests reuse the four reported failures, so they are not 250 independent trials. None of the 50 forward-only tests in analyses C and D was caught."),
("Platt scaling fitted on other failures gave a slope of exactly zero in 72 of 80","Platt scaling fitted on other failures gave a coefficient $b$ of exactly zero in 72 of 80"),
("and the largest slope for M5 and M6 was 0.095.","and the largest $b$ for M5 and M6 was 0.095."),
("Figure~\\ref{fig:onset} shows why. The compressor entered","Figure~\\ref{fig:onset} is consistent with this, although load is an operating state and does not show when a leak began. The compressor entered"),
("\\subsection{Further checks}\n","\\emph{Alarm definitions.} Counting only new notifications left registered catches at none of 250 and further-seed catches at 3 of 280, and reduced the catches with labels moved one hour later from 32 to 29. M3 at 1 hour caught three of four at that shift under both definitions, and the 11 further-interval catches were all new notifications. Valid decisions covered 96.7\\% of scored time. Per decision day, M6 at 30 minutes rose from 1.57 to 1.62 in analysis A, and no combination in any analysis changed between passing and failing the burden limit. Rebuilt alarms reproduced the frozen counts in 858 of 860 cells. In the other two, tied tree scores equal to the threshold were rounded above it when saved, which changed one false alarm each.\n\n\\subsection{Further checks}\n"),
("The result also bears on the data. Our conclusion depends on reported starts being approximately right, and the sensors support that to within about 25 minutes. Had reports lagged the physical onset by more, some of what we score as failure would be earlier than we assume, although a sustained loaded run so close to each start argues against a long lag on three of the four failures.",
"The result also bears on the data, and its limits should be clear. What we measured is that performance improved when labels included observations after the reported start. Sustained load transitions fell within 25 minutes of each reported start, but a load transition is an operating state and does not establish when a leak physically began. Our conclusion depends on reported starts being approximately right, and we cannot verify that from these data. If reports lagged the physical onset, some of what we score as warning time would already be failure, which would make warning harder to achieve, not easier to claim."),
(between("With each reported MetroPT-3 failure held out in turn, the logistic","\\section*{CRediT"),
"A model that ranks sensor records well is not yet a warning system that gives maintainers time to act at a tolerable false-alarm burden, and on MetroPT-3 the two diverged. Four reported failures, tested 250 times across models, horizons and analyses, gave no timely alarm from 30 minutes to 12 hours, including when training used only earlier data, and calibration fitted on other failures returned approximately the base rate. Four further seeds of the sequence models caught 3 of 280, with no repeat, and none met the rules. Counting only new notifications changed neither count. High rank scores on one failure fell when a neighbouring failure was also held out and were matched by shifting the label window. Explanations were steadier across seeds than across failures. The models fit their training failures closely, so the weakness is in transfer. Moving labels one hour into the reported failure gave near-perfect scores at 30 minutes and alarms almost entirely after the reported start, which is detection rather than warning. These findings apply to these models and this one compressor. They measure alarm burden and do not establish any maintenance benefit. Any claim of early warning should state how many failures it rests on, whether a catch means a new alert, and whether its labels reach into the failure.\n\n"),
]
R.append(("\\item Report alarms per unit of exposure across the full stream, not only over a window close to the failure, because alarm prevalence depends on how much normal operation is scored.\n","\\item Report alarms per unit of exposure across the full stream, not only over a window close to the failure, because alarm prevalence depends on how much normal operation is scored.\n\\item Define whether a catch means a new alert or a signal already active, and state whether the burden denominator includes time when no decision can be issued.\n"))
for a,b in R:
    assert T.count(a)==1,a[:70]
    T=T.replace(a,b)
p.write_text(T)
q=Path("submission-ress/highlights.txt");S=q.read_text()
a="- Holding out whole failures, registered alarms caught 0 of 250 failure tests";assert S.count(a)==1
q.write_text(S.replace(a,"- Alarms caught 0 of 250 failure tests, which reuse just four reported failures"))
print(len(R),"replacements")
